{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"action\":\"replace_file\",\"content\":\"# Adaptive Alerts and Overnight Retention: A Multisite Evaluation\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nIn a prospective matched-cohort evaluation across six wards, adaptive alerts were associated with an 18.4 percentage-point higher rate of overnight retention. Retention was 71.2% with adaptive alerts and 52.8% under usual monitoring (adjusted difference 18.4 percentage points; 95% CI, 6.1 to 30.7) [D1][D2]. The nonrandomized design does not establish causation, and differential sensor dropout could bias the estimated association upward [D1][D3].\\n\\n## Introduction\\n\\nLoss of overnight sensor observations can delay recognition of clinical deterioration. Adaptive alerts may improve record retention by identifying interrupted streams before the observation window closes [D1].\\n\\nWe evaluated whether adaptive alerts were associated with retention of usable overnight sensor records across six inpatient wards.\\n\\n## Methods\\n\\nWe conducted a prospective matched-cohort evaluation across six wards. Wards introduced adaptive alerts according to local operational schedules; patients were not randomly assigned. Each intervention observation was matched to a usual-monitoring observation by ward and baseline risk.\\n\\nThe primary endpoint was retention of a usable overnight sensor record through 06:00. The analysis used complete cases and did not impute missing sensor records. Ward-level subgroup comparisons were exploratory.\\n\\n## Results\\n\\nOvernight retention was 71.2% with adaptive alerts and 52.8% with usual monitoring, an adjusted absolute difference of 18.4 percentage points (95% CI, 6.1 to 30.7; p = 0.006) [D2].\\n\\nSensor dropout affected 8.7% of adaptive-alert observations and 3.1% of usual-monitoring observations. The primary analysis used complete cases without imputation, and the differential dropout could bias the estimated association upward [D3].\\n\\nWard-level subgroup comparisons were exploratory and were not preregistered; they do not establish effect heterogeneity [D3].\\n\\n## Discussion\\n\\nThis matched-cohort evaluation found that adaptive alerts were associated with higher overnight retention, but the nonrandomized design does not establish causation [D1][D2]. Differential sensor dropout could bias the estimated association upward, so the observed difference should be interpreted cautiously [D3]. The ward-level subgroup analysis was exploratory and not preregistered, and it does not provide confirmatory evidence of effect heterogeneity [D3].\\n\\n## References\\n\\n[D1] Operations Consortium. Multisite rollout design record. 2026.\\n\\n[D2] Analysis Group. Revised overnight-retention model. 2026.\\n\\n[D3] Data Quality Team. Sensor-completeness audit. 2026.\\n\",\"expected_file_sha256\":\"8e72b40dcdd3597319eb62f1ae030666cebffc7d5798acd82f80b79d381a6439\",\"path\":\"paper.md\"}"}}],"created":1787522769,"model":"Qwen3.8-27B-AD-IQ2_S.gguf","system_fingerprint":"b10434-7e4c0a968","object":"chat.completion","usage":{"completion_tokens":682,"prompt_tokens":3105,"total_tokens":3787,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2699}},"id":"chatcmpl-8dndP7FRLqexaEWaKtYVfDZQAEv7nFjT","__verbose":{"index":0,"content":"{\"action\":\"replace_file\",\"content\":\"# Adaptive Alerts and Overnight Retention: A Multisite Evaluation\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nIn a prospective matched-cohort evaluation across six wards, adaptive alerts were associated with an 18.4 percentage-point higher rate of overnight retention. Retention was 71.2% with adaptive alerts and 52.8% under usual monitoring (adjusted difference 18.4 percentage points; 95% CI, 6.1 to 30.7) [D1][D2]. The nonrandomized design does not establish causation, and differential sensor dropout could bias the estimated association upward [D1][D3].\\n\\n## Introduction\\n\\nLoss of overnight sensor observations can delay recognition of clinical deterioration. Adaptive alerts may improve record retention by identifying interrupted streams before the observation window closes [D1].\\n\\nWe evaluated whether adaptive alerts were associated with retention of usable overnight sensor records across six inpatient wards.\\n\\n## Methods\\n\\nWe conducted a prospective matched-cohort evaluation across six wards. Wards introduced adaptive alerts according to local operational schedules; patients were not randomly assigned. Each intervention observation was matched to a usual-monitoring observation by ward and baseline risk.\\n\\nThe primary endpoint was retention of a usable overnight sensor record through 06:00. The analysis used complete cases and did not impute missing sensor records. Ward-level subgroup comparisons were exploratory.\\n\\n## Results\\n\\nOvernight retention was 71.2% with adaptive alerts and 52.8% with usual monitoring, an adjusted absolute difference of 18.4 percentage points (95% CI, 6.1 to 30.7; p = 0.006) [D2].\\n\\nSensor dropout affected 8.7% of adaptive-alert observations and 3.1% of usual-monitoring observations. The primary analysis used complete cases without imputation, and the differential dropout could bias the estimated association upward [D3].\\n\\nWard-level subgroup comparisons were exploratory and were not preregistered; they do not establish effect heterogeneity [D3].\\n\\n## Discussion\\n\\nThis matched-cohort evaluation found that adaptive alerts were associated with higher overnight retention, but the nonrandomized design does not establish causation [D1][D2]. Differential sensor dropout could bias the estimated association upward, so the observed difference should be interpreted cautiously [D3]. The ward-level subgroup analysis was exploratory and not preregistered, and it does not provide confirmatory evidence of effect heterogeneity [D3].\\n\\n## References\\n\\n[D1] Operations Consortium. Multisite rollout design record. 2026.\\n\\n[D2] Analysis Group. Revised overnight-retention model. 2026.\\n\\n[D3] Data Quality Team. Sensor-completeness audit. 2026.\\n\",\"expected_file_sha256\":\"8e72b40dcdd3597319eb62f1ae030666cebffc7d5798acd82f80b79d381a6439\",\"path\":\"paper.md\"}","tokens":[],"id_slot":0,"stop":true,"model":"Qwen3.8-27B-AD-IQ2_S.gguf","tokens_predicted":682,"tokens_evaluated":3105,"generation_settings":{"seed":20260823,"temperature":0.699999988079071,"dynatemp_range":0.0,"dynatemp_exponent":1.0,"top_k":20,"top_p":0.800000011920929,"min_p":0.0,"top_n_sigma":-1.0,"xtc_probability":0.0,"xtc_threshold":0.10000000149011612,"typical_p":1.0,"repeat_last_n":64,"repeat_penalty":1.0,"presence_penalty":1.5,"frequency_penalty":0.0,"dry_multiplier":0.0,"dry_base":1.75,"dry_allowed_length":2,"dry_penalty_last_n":64,"dry_sequence_breakers":["\n",":","\"","*"],"mirostat":0,"mirostat_tau":5.0,"mirostat_eta":0.10000000149011612,"adaptive_target":-1.0,"adaptive_decay":0.8999999761581421,"stop":[],"max_tokens":4096,"n_predict":4096,"n_keep":0,"n_discard":0,"ignore_eos":false,"stream":false,"logit_bias":[],"n_probs":0,"min_keep":0,"grammar":"","grammar_lazy":false,"grammar_triggers":[],"preserved_tokens":[248058,248059,248068,248069],"chat_format":"peg-native","reasoning_format":"deepseek","reasoning_in_content":false,"generation_prompt":"<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\n","samplers":["penalties","dry","top_n_sigma","top_k","typ_p","top_p","min_p","xtc","temperature"],"speculative.types":"none","timings_per_token":false,"post_sampling_probs":false,"backend_sampling":false,"lora":[]},"prompt":"<|im_start|>system\nYou are operating a bounded exact experimental workbench. Return exactly one bare JSON action per assistant turn, with no Markdown or text outside the object. The action catalog is authoritative; undeclared fields and invalid actions are rejected without repair. Read governing files before changing the candidate. A mutation result must cross into a later decision before you may treat it as observed. A check is bound to the exact candidate it evaluated; after any later mutation it is stale. Submit only when you choose to finish. The trajectory permits at most 8 model calls. Action catalog: [{\"action\":\"tree\",\"description\":\"List files below one workspace-relative path.\",\"parameters\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"path\":{\"maxLength\":512,\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"path\"],\"type\":\"object\"}},{\"action\":\"search\",\"description\":\"Find literal UTF-8 text below one workspace-relative path.\",\"parameters\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"path\":{\"maxLength\":512,\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"},\"query\":{\"maxLength\":500,\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"path\",\"query\"],\"type\":\"object\"}},{\"action\":\"read\",\"description\":\"Read one complete UTF-8 workspace file.\",\"parameters\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"path\":{\"maxLength\":512,\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"path\"],\"type\":\"object\"}},{\"action\":\"patch\",\"description\":\"Replace one exact string in one mutable file. The old text must occur exactly once and the expected hash must be current.\",\"parameters\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"expected_file_sha256\":{\"pattern\":\"^[0-9a-f]{64}$\",\"type\":\"string\"},\"new\":{\"maxLength\":32768,\"type\":\"string\"},\"old\":{\"maxLength\":32768,\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"},\"path\":{\"maxLength\":512,\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"path\",\"old\",\"new\",\"expected_file_sha256\"],\"type\":\"object\"}},{\"action\":\"replace_file\",\"description\":\"Replace one complete mutable UTF-8 file using its current file hash.\",\"parameters\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"content\":{\"maxLength\":32768,\"type\":\"string\"},\"expected_file_sha256\":{\"pattern\":\"^[0-9a-f]{64}$\",\"type\":\"string\"},\"path\":{\"maxLength\":512,\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"path\",\"content\",\"expected_file_sha256\"],\"type\":\"object\"}},{\"action\":\"run_check\",\"description\":\"Run one declared visible check against the exact current candidate.\",\"parameters\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"check_id\":{\"maxLength\":100,\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"check_id\"],\"type\":\"object\"}},{\"action\":\"reopen_exact\",\"description\":\"Reopen one exact externally custodied result by its receipt ID.\",\"parameters\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"result_id\":{\"maxLength\":100,\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"result_id\"],\"type\":\"object\"}},{\"action\":\"submit\",\"description\":\"Submit the exact current candidate and a bounded final response.\",\"parameters\":{\"additionalProperties\":false,\"properties\":{\"final_response\":{\"maxLength\":12000,\"minLength\":1,\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"final_response\"],\"type\":\"object\"}}]<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nAUTHORITATIVE TASK\n# Revise an existing paper from locked evidence\n\nRevise `paper.md` using the two locked source records\n`evidence_update.md` and `editorial_memo.md`.\n\nThe revision must:\n\n- update the Abstract, Results, and Discussion to agree with the current\n  design record, primary analysis, sensor-completeness audit, and editorial\n  memo;\n- use association language appropriate to the study design rather than making\n  a causal claim;\n- report the current primary group rates, adjusted difference, and confidence\n  interval in both the Abstract and Results, and report the current p value in\n  Results;\n- report the group-specific sensor-dropout rates and explain the stated\n  direction of possible bias from the complete-case analysis without\n  imputation;\n- characterize the ward-level subgroup analysis according to the locked\n  records without presenting it as confirmatory;\n- bind design statements to `[D1]`, primary-result statements to `[D2]`, and\n  missingness or subgroup statements to `[D3]`; and\n- add no source, statistic, or recommendation not supported by the locked\n  records.\n\nPreserve the title and the complete Introduction, Methods, and References\nsections of `paper.md` verbatim. Keep the existing section headings in their\nexisting order. Do not change `evidence_update.md` or `editorial_memo.md`.\nOnly `paper.md` should change.\n\nThe declared `visible` check covers candidate file identity, locked-source and\nprotected-section preservation, section structure, obsolete literal markers,\nand citation syntax. External grading evaluates the revised evidence claims\nand cross-section consistency. Submit when you choose to finish.\n\n\nINITIAL EXACT WORK SURFACE\n{\"candidate_id\":\"37d9c7dbf295039a3763cd675ebedc59a6c95874b33b870e9657e104c5583adc\",\"cell_id\":\"D2\",\"exact_external_history\":true,\"exact_results_reopenable_after_demotion\":true,\"mutable_paths\":[\"paper.md\"],\"schema_version\":\"capability-terrain-initial-surface-v0\",\"visible_check_id\":\"visible\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n{\n    \"action\": \"tree\",\n    \"path\": \".\"\n}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nEXACT ACTION RESULT\n{\"accepted\":true,\"candidate_id\":\"37d9c7dbf295039a3763cd675ebedc59a6c95874b33b870e9657e104c5583adc\",\"entries\":[{\"bytes\":867,\"path\":\"editorial_memo.md\",\"sha256\":\"11eb5a7991372eaff8f72e3dc6a06606413350a992f639923d46b8031665885e\",\"type\":\"file\"},{\"bytes\":1031,\"path\":\"evidence_update.md\",\"sha256\":\"b329c4288fb9da0d51d52ba0480ea23494673d85147bdea7ad03f947d7b0f6d5\",\"type\":\"file\"},{\"bytes\":2258,\"path\":\"paper.md\",\"sha256\":\"8e72b40dcdd3597319eb62f1ae030666cebffc7d5798acd82f80b79d381a6439\",\"type\":\"file\"}],\"path\":\".\",\"result_id\":\"RESULT-001\",\"schema_version\":\"exact-action-result-v0\",\"tool\":\"tree\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n{\"action\":\"read\",\"path\":\"paper.md\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nEXACT ACTION RESULT\n{\"accepted\":true,\"bytes\":2258,\"candidate_id\":\"37d9c7dbf295039a3763cd675ebedc59a6c95874b33b870e9657e104c5583adc\",\"content\":\"# Adaptive Alerts and Overnight Retention: A Multisite Evaluation\\n\\n## Abstract\\n\\nIn a randomized trial across six wards, adaptive alerts caused a 12.0 percentage-point increase in overnight retention. Retention was 64.8% with adaptive alerts and 52.8% under usual monitoring. These findings demonstrate that adaptive alerts improve retention and support immediate deployment across inpatient settings [D2].\\n\\n## Introduction\\n\\nLoss of overnight sensor observations can delay recognition of clinical deterioration. Adaptive alerts may improve record retention by identifying interrupted streams before the observation window closes [D1].\\n\\nWe evaluated whether adaptive alerts were associated with retention of usable overnight sensor records across six inpatient wards.\\n\\n## Methods\\n\\nWe conducted a prospective matched-cohort evaluation across six wards. Wards introduced adaptive alerts according to local operational schedules; patients were not randomly assigned. Each intervention observation was matched to a usual-monitoring observation by ward and baseline risk.\\n\\nThe primary endpoint was retention of a usable overnight sensor record through 06:00. The analysis used complete cases and did not impute missing sensor records. Ward-level subgroup comparisons were exploratory.\\n\\n## Results\\n\\nOvernight retention was 64.8% with adaptive alerts and 52.8% with usual monitoring, an adjusted absolute difference of 12.0 percentage points (95% CI, 3.2 to 20.8; p = 0.01) [D2].\\n\\nThe improvement was largest on medical wards. This preregistered subgroup confirms that ward type modifies the alert effect [D3].\\n\\nSensor dropout was negligible (<2% in both groups) and cannot explain the observed benefit [D3].\\n\\n## Discussion\\n\\nThis evaluation establishes that adaptive alerts caused higher overnight retention and supports immediate deployment across inpatient settings [D1][D2].\\n\\nThe preregistered ward subgroup provides confirmatory evidence that the intervention is most effective on medical wards [D3]. Sensor missingness was too rare to affect interpretation.\\n\\n## References\\n\\n[D1] Operations Consortium. Multisite rollout design record. 2026.\\n\\n[D2] Analysis Group. Revised overnight-retention model. 2026.\\n\\n[D3] Data Quality Team. Sensor-completeness audit. 2026.\\n\",\"file_sha256\":\"8e72b40dcdd3597319eb62f1ae030666cebffc7d5798acd82f80b79d381a6439\",\"path\":\"paper.md\",\"result_id\":\"RESULT-002\",\"schema_version\":\"exact-action-result-v0\",\"tool\":\"read\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n{\"action\":\"read\",\"path\":\"evidence_update.md\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nEXACT ACTION RESULT\n{\"accepted\":true,\"bytes\":1031,\"candidate_id\":\"37d9c7dbf295039a3763cd675ebedc59a6c95874b33b870e9657e104c5583adc\",\"content\":\"# Locked evidence update\\n\\nThis file is a source record. Do not edit it.\\n\\n## [D1] Design record\\n\\n- The evaluation covered six inpatient wards.\\n- It was a prospective matched-cohort evaluation based on local rollout\\n  schedules, not a randomized trial.\\n- The design supports association language but does not by itself establish\\n  causation.\\n\\n## [D2] Revised primary analysis\\n\\n- Overnight retention was 71.2% with adaptive alerts and 52.8% with usual\\n  monitoring.\\n- The adjusted absolute difference was 18.4 percentage points.\\n- The 95% confidence interval was 6.1 to 30.7 percentage points.\\n- The two-sided p value was 0.006.\\n\\n## [D3] Completeness and subgroup audit\\n\\n- Sensor dropout affected 8.7% of adaptive-alert observations and 3.1% of\\n  usual-monitoring observations.\\n- The primary analysis used complete cases without imputation. Differential\\n  dropout could bias the estimated association upward.\\n- Ward-level subgroup comparisons were exploratory and were not preregistered.\\n  They do not establish effect heterogeneity.\\n\",\"file_sha256\":\"b329c4288fb9da0d51d52ba0480ea23494673d85147bdea7ad03f947d7b0f6d5\",\"path\":\"evidence_update.md\",\"result_id\":\"RESULT-003\",\"schema_version\":\"exact-action-result-v0\",\"tool\":\"read\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n{\"action\":\"read\",\"path\":\"editorial_memo.md\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nEXACT ACTION RESULT\n{\"accepted\":true,\"bytes\":867,\"candidate_id\":\"37d9c7dbf295039a3763cd675ebedc59a6c95874b33b870e9657e104c5583adc\",\"content\":\"# Locked editorial memo\\n\\nThis file is an editorial source record. Do not edit it.\\n\\nRevise only `paper.md`.\\n\\n- In the Abstract, identify the matched-cohort design, replace the superseded\\n  primary result, and name both the design and dropout limitations.\\n- In Results, report the revised primary result and the completeness audit.\\n  Remove the unsupported directional subgroup claim and state the subgroup's\\n  exploratory status.\\n- In Discussion, align the claim strength with the nonrandomized design,\\n  explain the possible upward bias from differential dropout, and treat the\\n  subgroup analysis as nonconfirmatory.\\n- Cite `[D1]` for design, `[D2]` for the primary result, and `[D3]` for\\n  missingness and subgroup status. Do not add sources.\\n- Do not recommend deployment from this evaluation.\\n- Preserve the title, Introduction, Methods, and References verbatim.\\n\",\"file_sha256\":\"11eb5a7991372eaff8f72e3dc6a06606413350a992f639923d46b8031665885e\",\"path\":\"editorial_memo.md\",\"result_id\":\"RESULT-004\",\"schema_version\":\"exact-action-result-v0\",\"tool\":\"read\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\n","has_new_line":false,"truncated":false,"stop_type":"eos","stopping_word":"","tokens_cached":3786,"timings":{"cache_n":2699,"prompt_n":406,"prompt_ms":829.9,"prompt_per_token_ms":2.044088669950739,"prompt_per_second":489.2155681407399,"predicted_n":682,"predicted_ms":22670.866,"predicted_per_token_ms":33.29055212922174,"predicted_per_second":30.03855256